WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.980
The Department of Defense depends on a broad industrial base to design, build, operate,

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:06.880
and support mission capabilities.

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:10.520
Sensitive information often moves across organizational boundaries as many companies

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:12.840
contribute to the same program or supply chain.

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:15.520
A weakness at any participant can create wider risk.

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:18.855
A small supplier may handle information that affects a major platform,

00:00:18.855 --> 00:00:23.295
while a service provider may support systems used by several contractors.

00:00:23.295 --> 00:00:26.176
Organization size alone does not determine information sensitivity.

00:00:26.176 --> 00:00:30.675
Cyber threats against the DIB can affect intellectual property, acquisition information,

00:00:30.675 --> 00:00:35.320
engineering data, operational capability, cost, schedule, and national security.

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:38.900
Protecting unclassified information is therefore an important part of protecting defense

00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:39.448
missions.

00:00:39.448 --> 00:00:42.265
DoD contracts have long included safeguarding requirements,

00:00:42.265 --> 00:00:45.655
including requirements connected to FCI, CUI, and NIST SP 800-171.

00:00:45.655 --> 00:00:52.100
CMMC adds a formal verification framework around applicable cybersecurity expectations.

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:55.490
Verification helps the government and contracting community distinguish between a stated

00:00:55.490 --> 00:00:58.459
policy and a practice that is implemented within a defined environment.

00:00:58.459 --> 00:01:03.560
It creates common terminology for levels, assessments, findings, affirmations, and status.

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:06.785
CMMC also supports supply-chain accountability.

00:01:06.785 --> 00:01:10.430
Requirements may affect subcontractors when protected information or covered contract work

00:01:10.430 --> 00:01:11.140
flows to them,

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:15.745
so prime contractors and suppliers need clear communication about information, systems,

00:01:15.745 --> 00:01:16.795
and responsibilities.

00:01:16.795 --> 00:01:18.990
The program is intended to improve consistency,

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:21.195
but it does not make every contractor identical.

00:01:21.195 --> 00:01:25.230
The required level and assessment type are determined by current program rules and the

00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:27.150
applicable solicitation or contract.

00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:31.250
CMMC cannot eliminate cyber threats or guarantee that an organization will never

00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:32.820
experience an incident.

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:35.390
Its value is stronger implementation, clearer accountability,

00:01:35.390 --> 00:01:39.000
and greater confidence that protected information receives required safeguards.
